{"word": "applause", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "applause", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/əˈplɔːz/", "tags": ["UK"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/əˈplɔz/", "tags": ["US"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/əˈplɑz/", "tags": ["cot caught merger"]}], "forms": [{"word": "applauses", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "The act of applauding; approbation and praise publicly expressed by the clapping of hands, stamping or tapping of the feet, acclamation, huzzas, or other means; marked commendation.", "tags": ["uncountable", "usually"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "A few days before, the adulations and applauses of a nation were sounding in her [Marie Louise, Duchess of Parma's] ears, and now she was come to this!", "reference": "1880, Mark Twain [pseudonym] (Samuel L[anghorne] Clemens), “”, in A Tramp Abroad; […], Hartford, Conn.: American Publishing Company; London: Chatto & Windus, →OCLC, page 535:"}, {"text": "Lestrade and I sat silent for a moment, and then, with a spontaneous impulse, we both broke at clapping, as at the well-wrought crisis of a play. […] It was at such moments that for an instant he [Sherlock Holmes] ceased to be a reasoning machine, and betrayed his human love for admiration and applause.", "reference": "1904 April 30, A[rthur] Conan Doyle, “The Adventure of the Six Napoleons”, in The Return of Sherlock Holmes, New York, N.Y.: McClure, Phillips & Co., published February 1905, →OCLC, page 232:"}, {"text": "Livy never gets her share of those applauses, but it is because the people do not know. Yet she is entitled to the lion's share.", "reference": "1916, Albert Bigelow Paine, chapter 57, in The Boys’ Life of Mark Twain:"}, {"text": "Rice’s celebrations upon his goal were controlled, muted. Not so those of Grealish, who lapped up the applause of the England fans behind the goal.", "reference": "2024 September 7, David H